---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

// An Introduction to Vegan Eating — its own section of An Orthodox
// Table, for catechumens and inquirers who have been told what the
// fast sets aside and not what to actually cook.
//
// Deliberately separate from Keeping the Fast, which answers what the
// fast asks of a person — the rule, and who to speak to if they cannot
// keep it. This page
// answers the other half: how to cook under it. The two link to each
// other and neither repeats the other.
//
// Written to grow. Each topic is its own <section> with an id, so a
// new one is added without disturbing what is here.

// What to keep in the house. Kept as data rather than written out as
// markup, so a
// line can be added without touching layout, the way the protein table
// on Keeping the Fast works.
//
// Brands are named on purpose: a person standing in an aisle wants to
// know what to reach for, not a principle. They are named WITH the
// warning that recipes change, because they do, and a parish website
// cannot keep up with them.
const onHand = [
  {
    group: "Canned",
    items: [
      "Beans — kidney, black, pinto, chickpea",
      "Tomato sauce and tomato paste",
      "Fruit and vegetables",
      "Coconut milk",
      "Soups — some Amy's and Well Yes! are vegan, but read the can",
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Dry goods",
    items: [
      "Pasta, rice, oats",
      "Lentils, beans, split peas",
      "Bread and tortillas",
      "Nuts and seeds",
      "Spices and salt",
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Baking",
    items: [
      "Flour, baking soda, baking powder, cornstarch",
      "Organic sugar — see Reading labels below",
      "Cocoa powder and vanilla extract",
      "Chocolate chips, dairy-free",
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Freezer",
    items: [
      "Fruit and vegetables",
      "Fries and diced potatoes",
      "Waffles — read the box, not all of them are vegan",
      "Ice cream — So Delicious, Ben & Jerry's non-dairy line",
      "Pizza — Daiya, some Amy's",
      "Frozen meals — Gardein, Sweet Earth",
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Oils, sauces and condiments",
    items: [
      "Olive oil, and a plain oil for frying",
      "Mustard, ketchup, vinegar",
      "Maple syrup",
      "Hot sauce — a surprising number are made with butter or cream",
      "Pasta sauce — check it, a good many are made with cheese",
      "Mayonnaise — Vegenaise, Just Mayo, Hellmann's vegan",
      "Peanut butter and jelly",
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Instead of dairy",
    items: [
      "Milk — oat, soy, almond, coconut, cashew",
      "Creamer — Silk, Nutpods, So Delicious, Califia",
      "Yogurt — Silk, So Delicious, Ripple, Chobani non-dairy, Kite Hill",
      "Cheese — Violife, Daiya, Follow Your Heart, Chao, So Delicious",
      "(a tub marked lactose-free may still be made of milk — the two words are not the same)",
      "Cream cheese — Tofutti, Kite Hill",
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Instead of meat",
    items: [
      "Tofu and tempeh",
      "Lentils and beans — by far the cheapest of these",
      "Ground beef substitutes and burgers — Impossible, Beyond, Gardein, MorningStar, Dr. Praeger's",
      "Bacon — Lightlife, Sweet Earth",
      "Sausage — Field Roast, Lightlife, Beyond, Gardein",
      "Chicken-style — Gardein, MorningStar",
    ],
  },
];

// The names animal products hide under, which is the part nobody
// tells you.
const hiddenNames = [
  { name: "Casein", what: "a milk protein" },
  { name: "Whey", what: "also milk" },
  { name: "Lactose", what: "milk sugar" },
  { name: "Gelatin", what: "boiled bone and hide — sweets, marshmallows, some yogurts" },
  { name: "Isinglass", what: "fish, used to clarify some beers and wines" },
  { name: "Carmine", what: "crushed insects, used as red coloring" },
  { name: "Lard, suet, tallow", what: "rendered animal fat" },
  {
    name: "Sugar filtered through bone char",
    what: "the label won't say — organic sugar never uses it, so buy organic when it matters",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="An Introduction to Vegan Eating | An Orthodox Table"
  description="How to cook for the Orthodox fast — what to keep in the house, how to read a label, and how to cook tofu so you actually like it. St. John of Kronstadt Orthodox Mission, Bayview TX."
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 md:px-6">
    <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">
      <a href="/table/" class="text-brand-tan-dark no-underline hover:underline">← An Orthodox Table</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl text-text-primary md:text-4xl">
      An Introduction to Vegan Eating
    </h1>
    <div class="mt-5 h-px w-24 bg-liturgical-current"></div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
      Nearly half the year is a fast, and for most of it that means no meat,
      dairy or eggs. The rule itself is simple enough. The hard part is
      standing in your kitchen on a Wednesday night with no idea what to
      make.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
      So this page is about the cooking. What the fast actually asks of you,
      and who to talk to if you can't keep it, is on
      <a href="/table/keeping-the-fast/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">Keeping the Fast</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- A page this long needs a way in. -->
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="mt-8 border border-border-soft bg-cream-card p-5">
      <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">On this page</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-1 text-sm sm:grid-cols-2">
        <li><a href="#start-here" class="text-brand-blue-dark">Where to begin</a></li>
        <li><a href="#try-things" class="text-brand-blue-dark">Then add to it</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tofu" class="text-brand-blue-dark">Tofu, done properly</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cost" class="text-brand-blue-dark">What it costs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#on-hand" class="text-brand-blue-dark">What to keep in the house</a></li>
        <li><a href="#labels" class="text-brand-blue-dark">Reading labels</a></li>
        <li><a href="#vitamins" class="text-brand-blue-dark">Vitamins and B12</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <!-- Theia's words. The order is the point: the reframing comes
         first, because the obstacle is how the fast is being thought
         about rather than what is in the house. Only once it is a
         small thing freely given does the practical business of lists
         and planning make any sense. -->
    <section id="start-here" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Where to begin</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Changing how you have been eating for most or all of your life can feel
        overwhelming. Almost like being asked to give up everything at once. The
        trick is changing your mindset and reframing the &ldquo;problem&rdquo;.
        Instead of thinking of the fast as something being taken from you, first
        start with acknowledging that it is a small voluntary sacrifice from you
        to God. He gave us His Son; we give Him a day or a month where we don't
        eat animal products. Now we can start to see that our sacrifice is small
        comparatively speaking, but because we are human and we do struggle with
        this, God sees our efforts.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        The fastest way to see that it is a small change, rather than a
        life-altering choice, is to notice how much of what you already eat is
        fasting food: Pasta. Peanut butter and jelly. Rice and beans. Potatoes.
        Berries and fruits. Most chips and crackers. Oatmeal. Hummus and pita.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Write your favorite non-animal derived foods down on a list — on your
        phone, or on the fridge, somewhere you will see it. In the evening when
        you are tired and certain there is nothing in the house to eat, the list
        is the answer, and you don't even have to think too hard about it. Even
        better, we are adding some
        <a href="/table/fasting/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">vegan recipes</a>.
        When you know a fast is coming up you can
        <a href="/calendar/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">check the calendar</a>,
        see how long it is and plan out your meals — and be on the lookout for
        fish days! You can do this. Planning, knowledge, and support from your
        fellow parishioners will help get you across the line.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="try-things" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Then add to it</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Fasting is a good excuse to try food you'd never have bothered with. A
        lot of Indian, Ethiopian, Greek and Middle Eastern cooking is already
        vegan — not changed to be, just made that way. Cook a vegetable a way
        you never have. Try that restaurant you keep driving past.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        When something turns out good, add it to your list. Before long you'll
        have more than you can cook in a week, and the list is what you shop
        from. Some people keep three: what the family will actually eat, what's
        fast on a weeknight, and what's worth the effort on a Sunday.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Tofu early, because it is the thing most likely to decide
         whether somebody keeps fasting or gives up on it. Almost
         everyone who says they dislike tofu has only met it
         waterlogged — a texture problem, and a fixable one. Somebody
         who learns to press and crisp it has gained a weeknight
         replacement for chicken; somebody who doesn't tends to
         conclude the fast means plain rice.

         Both recipes sit in the fasting book in full. The method is
         repeated here because a person reading an introduction should
         not have to open two more pages to find out what to do. -->
    <section id="tofu" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Tofu, and why you may have had it badly</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Most people who say they don't like tofu have only ever eaten it
        straight from the packet. That is not a taste problem, it is a
        texture one. Tofu is sold sitting in water, and until that water
        is out it will not brown, it will not take up a marinade, and it
        stays soft in a way that is genuinely unpleasant to eat. Pressed
        and then crisped, it is a different food — firm, browned at the
        edges, and a real stand-in for chicken or beef in a stir fry, a
        taco or a bowl.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Learn it once, early. It's the difference between a fast of plain rice
        and a fast you can actually keep.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="border border-border-soft bg-cream-card p-5">
          <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">Step one</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-text-primary">Press it</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            Buy <strong>firm or extra firm</strong> — never regular or silken,
            which fall apart. Drain it, then get the water out one of two ways:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            <li>
              <strong>With a press:</strong> tighten a little, stand it over a
              bowl, and give it a few more turns every five minutes, flipping
              it so both sides drain. Tightening hard all at once makes the
              block bulge at the sides.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Without one:</strong> lay the block — or slices, which
              press better — on a folded towel on a board, put another towel
              and board on top, and weigh it down with a cast-iron pan or a
              few cans. Enough weight to squeeze, not enough to crush. Swap the
              towels when they get soaked.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            Thirty minutes is the minimum. An hour is better, and it can sit in
            the fridge overnight.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            <a
              href="/table/recipe/?slug=how-to-press-tofu"
              class="font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-blue-dark no-underline hover:underline"
            >
              The full method →
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-border-soft bg-cream-card p-5">
          <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">Step two</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-text-primary">Crisp it</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            Cut the pressed block into cubes. Toss them in soy sauce, then in a
            spoonful each of flour and corn starch with a little garlic powder,
            until every side is coated.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            Get the oil properly hot — <strong>the tofu should sizzle the
            moment it goes in</strong>. Lay the cubes in one layer, leave them
            alone three to five minutes until browned, then turn and brown the
            other side. Crowding the pan steams them instead, which undoes the
            pressing.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            A non-stick pan or a well-seasoned cast-iron skillet. Salt at the
            end if it needs it.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            <a
              href="/table/recipe/?slug=crispy-tofu-pan-fried"
              class="font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-blue-dark no-underline hover:underline"
            >
              The full recipe →
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-text-secondary">
        Once you have those two, the tofu dishes in the
        <a href="/table/fasting/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">fasting book</a>
        — fajitas, tacos, scramble, teriyaki — all start from the same place.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cost" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">What it costs</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        If you've looked at the vegan shelf and seen what the cheese and the
        fake ground beef cost, don't let it put you off — but know you don't
        need any of it. That stuff is convenience, and it's the most expensive
        way to fast. Buy it when you want to, not because you have to.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        The cheap way is also the old way. Instead of swapping the beef in your
        spaghetti sauce for vegan beef, make it with lentils. Rice, vegetables
        and beans costs almost nothing, and it's what most of the fasting world
        has always eaten. Dried beans and lentils, a store with a good bulk
        section, and comparing prices at two or three stores will do more for
        your grocery bill than anything else on this page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="on-hand" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">What to keep in the house</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Not a list to buy in one trip. Keep a few things from each row and most
        of what is in the fasting book is already something you can make.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-7">
        {
          onHand.map((row) => (
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">
                {row.group}
              </h3>
              <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
                {row.items.map((item) => (
                  <li class="border-b border-border-soft pb-1">{item}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-text-secondary">
        We name brands because standing in an aisle you want to know what to
        grab. Companies change their recipes though, and we can't keep up with
        them — so always check the label.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="labels" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Reading labels</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Animal products hide under names that don't sound like animals, and
        that's the part nobody warns you about. These are the ones worth knowing
        on sight.
      </p>

      <dl class="mt-5 space-y-2">
        {
          hiddenNames.map((row) => (
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 border-b border-border-soft pb-2">
              <dt class="font-semibold text-text-primary">{row.name}</dt>
              <dd class="text-sm text-text-secondary">{row.what}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <!-- Our rule and the internet's rule differ here, and somebody
           following generic vegan advice will refuse food they could
           have eaten. Worth saying plainly. -->
      <div class="mt-6 border-l-2 border-sacred-gold bg-cream-card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-base text-text-primary">Honey is allowed</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
          Strict vegan guidance excludes honey, and most of the advice you will
          find online does. Our fast does not. What is set aside is the flesh
          and produce of animals with bones, and bees are not among them. So a
          barbecue sauce that lists honey is a problem for a vegan and not for
          you — worth knowing before you put back something you never needed to.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="vitamins" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Vitamins and B12</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        B12 comes almost entirely from animal foods, and a long fast is the one
        thing on this page worth asking about rather than reading about. A
        multivitamin and a B12 supplement are what most people take.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Speak with your doctor, and with Fr.
        Antonios.</strong> A website does not know your bloodwork and cannot
        give you a rule. Between the two of them you will get an answer that
        fits you, and if Fr. Antonios sets you a different rule, that is his to
        set.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14 border-l-2 border-sacred-gold bg-cream-card p-5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-base text-text-primary">More of this is coming</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
        This section is being written as the parish works out what newcomers
        most need to know. If there is something you wished somebody had told
        you in your first Lent, tell the parish office — it probably belongs
        here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="mt-10 border-t border-border-soft pt-6 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      The recipes themselves are in the
      <a href="/table/fasting/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">fasting book</a>,
      open to anyone. What the fast asks, and who to speak to if you cannot
      keep it, is on
      <a href="/table/keeping-the-fast/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">Keeping the Fast</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
